window.docContents[37].resolve({"/Appendix/#section-chapter-9-subgraphs":{"contents":"* Subgraph.mycielskianSubgraph: lift of a subgraph in G to a subgraph in mμ[G].* matchingNum / ν G: matching number of G (in ℕ∞, mirroring\n  SimpleGraph.vertexCoverNum), the maximum number of edges in a matching of G.* Subgraph.boxProdLeft: a subgraph M of G copied into every H-fibre of\n  G □ H.* hypercube.matching: perfect matching of Q[n + 1], defined recursively as ⊤ on\n  Q[1] and the fibre-copy of the previous matching thereafter.* Subgraph.mycielskianDouble: the subgraph of mμ[G] with edges x ₗ — y ᵣ and\n  x ᵣ — y ₗ for each edge x — y of a subgraph of G.* Subgraph.mycielskianDoubleDartEmbedding: embedding of the darts of a subgraph\n  into the edge set of its Mycielskian double.* ColoringOn: proper colouring of a subgraph induced by a subset of vertices.* ColorableOn: predicate for a subset of vertices being colourable.* coloringOnEmpty: the colouring of the empty set of vertices by a default colour.* ColoringOn.restrict: restriction of a G.ColoringOn γ s to any t ⊆ s.* ColoringOn.copy: transport a G.ColoringOn γ s along a proof that s = t.* ColoringOn.toColoring: a G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ is a G.Coloring γ.* Coloring.toColoringOn: the ColoringOn of a set s induced by a colouring of\n  the whole graph.* coloringOnOfSingleton: colouring of {a} by any colour c.* coloringOnOfNotAdj: colouring of {a, b} by a single colour c when\n  ¬ G.Adj a b.* ColoringOn.union: combination of compatible colourings of s and t into a\n  colouring of s ∪ t.* ColoringOn.insert: extension of a colouring of s to insert a s using a colour\n  not used on neighbours of a in s.* ColoringOn.map: relabelling of the colours of a partial colouring along an\n  injective map.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"14.2.8. Chapter 9: Subgraphs","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-9-subgraphs"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-computations":{"contents":"As well as reasoning about graphs in Lean we can also do computations with them. For\nexample we can check that 0 and 3 are adjacent in the complete graph on Fin 5\n(the type consisting of {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}).\n\n#eval (⊤ : SimpleGraph (Fin 5)).Adj 0 3   -- true\n\n\nWe can count edges in finite graphs. For example the completeGraph on Fin 6 has\n15 edges.\n\n#eval (⊤ : SimpleGraph (Fin 6)).edgeFinset.card -- 15\n\n\nEven when our goal is to prove theorems, computations such as these can be very\nuseful. Firstly, they allow us to perform quick sanity checks of our definitions\nand secondly they can be used inside proofs with the decide tactic.\n\nexample : (⊤ : SimpleGraph (Fin 6)).Adj 2 3 := by decide\n\n\nBoth #eval and decide rely on adjacency being decidable: Lean needs a concrete\nmethod for deciding whether or not any given pair of vertices are adjacent. We will\nreturn to this, and to computations with graphs more generally, in\nDecidability, Finiteness and Degrees.\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\nnamespace SimpleGraph\nvariable {V : Type*}\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph","header":"2.7. Computations","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-computations"},"/Walks/#section-cycle-graphs-and-complete-graphs-are-hamiltonian":{"contents":"Having walked along the path graph from end to end we can wrap this Hamiltonian path\naround in the cycle graph to give a Hamiltonian cycle. The path graph P[n + 3] is\na subgraph of C[n + 3] (pathGraph_le_cycleGraph in Mathlib) so Walk.transfer\nmoves pathGraphWalk into C[n + 3], where the wrap-around edge from 0 to\nFin.last (n + 2) closes it into a Hamiltonian cycle. (Notice that we have used two\nof our plumbing tools here: both Walk.copy and Walk.transfer.)\n\n/-- The walk once around C[n + 3], built by transferring pathGraphWalk and\nclosing it with the wrap-around edge. -/\ndef Walk.cycleGraphCycle (n : ℕ) : C[n + 3].Walk 0 0 :=\n  have adj : C[n + 3].Adj 0 (Fin.last (n + 2)) := by simp [cycleGraph_adj]\n  Walk.cons adj (((pathGraphWalk (n + 2)).transfer C[n + 3]\n    (fun e he => edgeSet_mono pathGraph_le_cycleGraph\n      ((pathGraphWalk _).edges_subset_edgeSet he))).reverse)\n\n\nThis walk is a cycle: the transferred path is still a path, so it only remains to\ncheck that the closing edge is not an edge of the path, which holds if its length is\nnot equal to 1 (which is true).\n\nlemma Walk.cycleGraphCycle_isCycle (n : ℕ) : (cycleGraphCycle n).IsCycle := by\n  apply (cons_isCycle_iff_length_ne_one _ _).2\n          ⟨(pathGraphWalk_isPath.transfer _).reverse, _⟩\n  simp\n\n\nSince it is a cycle of length n + 3 through the n + 3 vertices of C[n + 3],\nit is a Hamiltonian cycle, and so cycle graphs (on at least three vertices) are\nHamiltonian.\n\nlemma Walk.cycleGraphCycle_isHamiltonianCycle (n : ℕ) :\n    (cycleGraphCycle n).IsHamiltonianCycle := by\n  rw [isHamiltonianCycle_iff_isCycle_and_length_eq]\n  exact ⟨cycleGraphCycle_isCycle n, by simp [cycleGraphCycle]⟩\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma cycleGraph_isHamiltonian (n : ℕ) : C[n + 3].IsHamiltonian :=\n  fun _ => ⟨_, _, Walk.cycleGraphCycle_isHamiltonianCycle n⟩\n\n\nMathlib also has its own walk once around C[n + 3], cycleGraph.cycle, built by a\ndirect recursion on the vertices rather than by transferring a path, together with\nthe facts that it is a cycle of length n + 3. (We built cycleGraphCycle here as a\nworked example of Walk.transfer; in later chapters we will use Mathlib's\ncycleGraph.cycle.)\n\n#check cycleGraph.cycle         -- (cycleGraph (n + 3)).Walk 0 0\n#check cycleGraph.isCycle_cycle -- (cycleGraph.cycle n).IsCycle\n#check cycleGraph.length_cycle  -- (cycleGraph.cycle n).length = n + 3\n\n\nWe give two easy but useful consequences. Firstly, Hamiltonicity is monotone: if\nG ≤ H and G has a Hamiltonian cycle then the same cycle is a Hamiltonian cycle in\nH (IsHamiltonian.mono). Since C[n + 3] ≤ ⊤ this tells us\nthat the complete graph K[n + 3] is Hamiltonian.\n\nlemma completeGraph_isHamiltonian (n : ℕ) :\n    K[n + 3].IsHamiltonian := (cycleGraph_isHamiltonian n).mono le_top\n\n\nSecondly, being Hamiltonian is obviously preserved by isomorphism, so any complete\ngraph on at least three vertices is Hamiltonian: we transport the Hamiltonian cycle\nof K[n + 3] along an isomorphism K[n + 3] ≃g ⊤ built from an equivalence\nFin (n + 3) ≃ V using Iso.completeGraph. (This innocent-looking lemma will be\nuseful in the proof of the Bondy–Chvátal theorem below.)\n\n#check Iso.completeGraph\n\n\nlemma top_isHamiltonian [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] (hn : 3 ≤ Fintype.card V) :\n    (⊤ : SimpleGraph V).IsHamiltonian := by\n  obtain ⟨n, hcard⟩ : ∃ n, Fintype.card V = n + 3 :=\n    ⟨Fintype.card V - 3, by grind⟩\n  -- since K[n + 3] is Hamiltonian it contains a Hamiltonian cycle\n  obtain ⟨a, c, hc⟩ := completeGraph_isHamiltonian n (by simp)\n  -- which we transport along an isomorphism K[n + 3] ≃g (⊤ : SimpleGraph V).\n  have e : Fin (n + 3) ≃ V := (Fintype.equivFinOfCardEq hcard).symm\n  intro\n  exact ⟨e a, c.map (Iso.completeGraph e).toHom, hc.map e.bijective⟩\n\n\nend basics\n\n\nsection hypercube\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Mapping Walks","header":"8.6.1. Cycle Graphs and Complete Graphs Are Hamiltonian","id":"/Walks/#section-cycle-graphs-and-complete-graphs-are-hamiltonian"}});